The surname Farmer is of occupational ong1l1 and derived its name
from the farmer, one who cultivated a farm. In French, the name is
Fermier and in Dutch the name is Vahrmeyer, both personal names.
This name comes from the Sa:>con term, Fearme or Feorme, whi.:::h
signifies food or provision . . . . however, some think it derived
from Firma in corrupt Latin, which signifies a place enclosed or shut
in, and some contend for its French etymology from the word Ferme.
The Farmers as far as our researches will enable us to conjecture were
Saxon origin, and in t'he reign of Edward 4th, King of England, were
seated in Northampton where they probably remain to this day.
It's principal homes in England were Devonshire, Kent, Leicestershire,
The name Farmer (as a given name) is of 'feuton:.::: origin, meaning
one Who tills its field.
Early records of the name are: Johannes Spenser (Firmarius or
Farmhand) 1379 (P. T. Yorks).
Thomas Fermour (husband) 1379 (P. T. Howdenshire).
Robertus Ryvyll (Fermer) 1379 ( P. T. Y-orks).
John Farmer, married Elizabeth Randall in 1S7S (Marriage License
London).
Francis Farmer, married Mary Willson in 1722 (St. Mary Aldermary).
Descendants:
Anthony Farmer, living in 1687, President - designate of Magdalen
College Oxford; received his M. A. degree in 1680; joined Magdalen
Co.llege in 168-~; was a scholar at Trinity College, Cambridge in 1676.
George Farmer (1732-1779) Navy Captain; Lieut. in the West Indies
and on home station; assisted in suppressing riot at Norwich in 1766;
appointed commander in 1768; perished in the burning of his ship
during the encounter with the French in 1779.
Hugh Farmer (1714 - 1787) Independent minister and theological
'writer; afternoon preacher at Salters' Hall in 1761 - 72; was a trustee
of Dr. vVilliams Foundations in 1762; published theological works.
John Farmer, living in 1591 - 1601, composer; dedk:ated to his friend
Edward deVere, 17th Earl of Oxford, a book containing examples in
two-part counter point of different orders in 1591.
Richard Farmer (1735 - 1797) master of Emmanuel College, Cam­bridge;
received his M. A. degree in 1775; Vice-Chance.llor of Cambridge
in 177S - 76; was prebendary and Chancellor of Lichfield in 1780; in­troduced
statuary into St. Paul's in 1788.
Thomas Farmer (1685) composer; Mus. Bac. at Cambridge in 1864;
contributed to muskal collections; published "consorts" in 1686 and 1690.
We also find a Richard Farmer of Easton in 1480. Richard Farmer
had a son, Edward and a daughter, Anna. Anna married about 1545 to
William Lucy and had a son, Sir Thomas Lucy of Charlecote, who was
knighted by Queen Elizabeth in 1565. He was the knight and magistrate
whose name is associated with some of the early events of the life of
Shakespeare.
Edward Farmer had a son, Bartholomew. Bartholomew Farmer had a
son, John Ansley Farmer, his wife's name was Isabe.lla. Their children's
names were Edward, John Hatton, and Thomas.
John Farmer a soldier in Moseley's company fought in the great fight
of Philip's war December 19, 1675. He married first, it is believed,
Elizabeth Hunt.
Hatton Farmer was killed in the battle in 1645. He was a major oi
Prince Charles Regt.
Thomas Farmer was in Billerica Mass. from 1675 to 1684.
Edward Farmer lived near Atherstone, Warwichshire England, until
he and his mother imigrated to America in 1671 settling in Billerica,
Mass.
